          "verdict": "plausible",
          "derivation": "NP لاغر lāḡar 'lean, thin, emaciated' is referred by Mann (IEC col. 700) to the nasalised r-stem *l̥ngʷʰ-ro- 'slight, nimble' of this root, the same formation as Gk. ἐλαφρός 'light, nimble' and OHG lungar 'swift': PIE *h₁ln̥gʷʰ-ró- > PIr. *Hlagra- > MP *lāgar > NP lāḡar, the semantic step being 'light (in weight)' → 'thin, lean' (compare the reverse of Pers. فربه farbeh 'fat', with which Mo'in explicitly contrasts it). The chain is phonologically awkward on two counts and that is why the verdict is not 'confirmed': (a) Old Iranian merged PIE *l into *r (hence the regular Avestan reflex ragu-, not *lagu-), so an inherited word could not keep initial l-; (b) the غ (ḡ/γ) for an expected -g- is likewise irregular. Neither Horn nor Hübschmann has the word, MacKenzie has no Pahlavi *lāgar, and Mo'in's footnote in Borhan-e Qate' gives only Iranian-dialect cognates (Kurdish, Natanzi, Sangsari, Lasgerdi, Shahmirzadi lāġer), which prove the word is old and widespread in Iranian but say nothing about its ultimate origin. Wiktionary's chain (MP *lāgar < OP *lagrah < PIr. *Hlagráh < PIE *h₁ln̥gʷʰrós) reproduces exactly this reconstruction but cites Mann and Nourai himself, so it is not independent confirmation.",
